<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mapuche</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Araucanians</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Mapuche are an indigenous people of south-central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chile">Chile</a> and south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argentina">Argentina</a>. They resisted Spanish conquest in the long <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arauco-war">Arauco War</a>, keeping their independence south of the Biobío river after 1598, and dealt with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> through treaties and parliaments. Chile and Argentina conquered their lands only in the 1860s–1880s; they remain the largest indigenous people of Chile.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mapuche.jpg" alt="Mapuche" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Al2 · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ancient_mapuche_flag.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Mapuche are a group of Indigenous inhabitants of south-central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chile">Chile</a> and south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argentina">Argentina</a>, who share a common social, religious and economic structure and the Mapudungun language. Archaeological finds show that Mapuche culture existed in Chile and Argentina as early as 600 to 500 BCE. At the time of the Spanish arrival the Picunche lived in the valleys between the Choapa and Itata rivers, the Mapuche proper between the Itata and Toltén, and the Huilliche and Cunco as far south as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiloe">Chiloé Archipelago</a>.</p><p>Troops of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca Empire</a> are reported to have reached the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maule-river">Maule River</a> and fought the Mapuche between the Maule and the Itata. Most modern scholars place the southern border of the Inca Empire between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago">Santiago</a> and the Maipo River, or somewhere between Santiago and the Maule, so the bulk of the Mapuche escaped Inca rule.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Spanish expansion into Mapuche territory was an offshoot of the conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>. In 1536 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diego-de-almagro">Diego de Almagro</a> set out to conquer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chile">Chile</a> but was met at the Battle of Reynogüelén by a large Mapuche force armed with bows and pikes, and he retreated to Peru the following year. In 1541 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pedro-de-valdivia">Pedro de Valdivia</a> founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago">Santiago</a>. The Picunche leader Michimalonco led resistance to the Spanish between 1541 and 1545, and his best-known achievement is the destruction of Santiago. The Mapuche learned horse riding and cavalry warfare from the Spaniards.</p><p>In the 17th to 19th centuries Mapuche groups migrated eastward into the Andes and Pampas, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tehuelche">Tehuelche</a> adopted the Mapuche language and some of their culture, in a process called Araucanization. Mapuche society in Araucanía and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/patagonia">Patagonia</a> remained independent until the late 19th century. Between 1861 and 1879 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chile">Chile</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argentina">Argentina</a> incorporated several Mapuche territories, and in January 1881, after Chile had defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>, the conquest resumed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The traditional economy is based on agriculture, and the traditional social organisation consists of extended families led by a lonko, or chief. In wartime the Mapuche united in larger groups and elected a toki, meaning axe-bearer, as leader. Mapuche cosmology centres on a creator, ngenechen, who takes the form of an older man, an older woman, a young man and a young woman. The best-known ceremony is the Ngillatun, a communal prayer.</p><p>Textiles are among their best-known arts. Women spun and wove, with skills passed on by mothers, grandmothers and aunts, and a man was expected to give a larger dowry for a bride who was an accomplished weaver. From the later 18th century Mapuche silversmiths made large amounts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> jewellery, working Spanish, Argentine and Chilean coins into headdresses called trarilonko. Mapudungun is spoken in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chile">Chile</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argentina">Argentina</a>, and about 92 per cent of Mapuche live in Chile.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mapuche” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…and then, in the Conquest of the Desert led by General Julio Argentino Roca in 1878–1879 and continued into the mid-1880s, used Remington rifles, the telegraph and railways to overrun the lands of the <b>Mapuche</b>, Ranquel and Tehuelche peoples, killing and displacing thousands and opening Patagonia to settlement.</blockquote><a href="/empire/argentine-republic">Argentine Republic · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Túpac Inca Yupanqui defeated the Kingdom of Chimor around 1470 and campaigned south through Collasuyu into present-day Chile and north-west Argentina, where the advance stopped near the Maule River in the face of <b>Mapuche</b> resistance.</blockquote><a href="/empire/inca-empire">Inca Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>At home the army completed the occupation of Araucanía, subduing the independent <b>Mapuche</b> by 1883.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-chile">Republic of Chile · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Pampas and Patagonia were <b>Mapuche</b>, Ranquel and Puelche country, the Gran Chaco Guaycuru.</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-provinces-of-the-rio-de-la-plata">United Provinces of the Río de la Plata · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>On the southern frontier the Spanish in Chile fought a long war against the <b>Mapuche</b>, financed by an annual subsidy, the real situado, sent from Lima.</blockquote><a href="/empire/viceroyalty-of-peru">Viceroyalty of Peru · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Aspects of Andean religion reach beyond the empire's former borders to the <b>Mapuches</b> and Huilliches of southern Chile, which has raised the hypothesis of an earlier spread of Andean religion from Tiwanaku.</blockquote><a href="/religion/inca-religion">Inca religion · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
